<GuidePage
  slug="extensions/filters"
  lead="Filters are the controls in a source's search sheet. Katari draws them and keeps their state; the source declares them and turns them into a search."
>
  <Task id="controls" title="The controls">
    <p>
      A filter is a class extending one of the controls below, all nested in <code>EntryFilter</code>. Katari changes its
      <code>state</code> as the user picks, and passes the whole list to <code>getSearchContent</code>.
    </p>
    <Members>
      <Member signature="abstract class Select<V>(name: String, values: Array<V>, state: Int = 0)">
        One choice from <code>values</code>. The state is the index of the choice.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="abstract class Sort(name: String, values: Array<String>, state: Selection? = null)">
        A sort order and its direction. The state is a <code>Sort.Selection(index, ascending)</code>, or null for the site's default.
      </Member>
      <Member signature={`abstract class Text(name: String, state: String = "")`}>A text field. The state is the text.</Member>
      <Member signature="abstract class CheckBox(name: String, state: Boolean = false)">
        An on/off switch. The state is <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="abstract class TriState(name: String, state: Int = 0)">
        A choice to include, exclude or ignore, such as one genre. The state is <code>STATE_IGNORE</code> (0),
        <code>STATE_INCLUDE</code> (1) or <code>STATE_EXCLUDE</code> (2), also read with <code>isIgnored()</code>,
        <code>isIncluded()</code> and <code>isExcluded()</code>.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="abstract class Group<V>(name: String, state: List<V>)">
        A titled, collapsible set of other filters. The state is the list of filters inside it.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="open class Header(name: String)">A heading between filters. It has no state.</Member>
      <Member signature={`open class Separator(name: String = "")`}>A line between filters. It has no state.</Member>
    </Members>
    <p>
      Three more are covered below: <code>EntryDateFilter</code> for <a href="#dates">dates</a>, and <code>Autocomplete</code> and
      <code>PagedGroup</code> for <a href="#site-options">options the site provides</a> and
      <a href="#paged-group">long lists</a>. <code>EntryFilter</code> and
      <code>EntryFilterList</code> are in <code>eu.kanade.tachiyomi.source.entry</code>; the metadata, dates and validation on this page
      are in <code>eu.kanade.tachiyomi.source.entry.filter</code>.
    </p>
    <p>Here are Example Comics' filters. The metadata in each is explained in the next sections:</p>
    <Snippet lang="kotlin" code={declare} />
    <p>
      <code>getFilterList</code> returns a new list each time. Katari works on its own copies of the filters and sets the user's values
      on yours only while it calls <code>getSearchContent</code>:
    </p>
    <Snippet lang="kotlin" code={filterList} />
    <p>
      In <code>getSearchContent</code>, read each filter's state and turn it into the site's parameters. The option IDs from the metadata
      make good parameter values:
    </p>
    <Snippet lang="kotlin" code={read} />
    <p>
      An exception thrown by a filter while Katari draws, counts or checks it affects only that filter: Katari carries on without its
      summary and checks, and says so under it.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="presets" title="Keep saved presets working">
    <p>
      Users can save a set of filter values as a <strong>filter preset</strong>, and feeds in <strong>Browse › Feeds</strong> are built on
      them. Without metadata, Katari finds a saved filter again by its label and its position among the filters, and a choice by its
      index. Renaming a filter, translating its label, moving it, or adding a choice before others then breaks the presets that use it,
      and the user is asked to repair each one.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>EntryFilterMetadataProvider</code> gives filters an identity that doesn't depend on how they look. Give one to every filter
      from the first release, since adding it later takes the extra steps below. <code>EntryFilterMetadata</code> holds:
    </p>
    <Members>
      <Member signature="val id: String? = null">
        Names the filter. Unique across all of the source's filters, including those inside groups, and never reused for something else.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="val optionIds: List<String> = emptyList()">
        Names each choice of a <code>Select</code> or <code>Sort</code>, in the same order as <code>values</code>. Reorder the choices
        freely; the IDs move with them.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="val neutralOptionId: String? = null">
        The choice that means no filtering, as in <a href="#counting">Active filters and clearing</a>.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="val role: EntryFilterRole = EntryFilterRole.CONSTRAINT">
        <code>ORDERING</code> for a <code>Select</code> that sets the order, as in <a href="#counting">Active filters and clearing</a>.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="val description: String? = null">A line of help under the filter.</Member>
      <Member signature="val aliases: Set<String> = emptySet()">Former IDs of this filter, such as those of filters merged into it.</Member>
      <Member signature="val optionAliases: Map<String, String> = emptyMap()">
        Maps former choice IDs to current ones in <code>optionIds</code>.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="val legacyNames: Set<String> = emptySet()">
        The labels the filter had in releases without an <code>id</code>, for presets saved then.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="val legacyOptionIds: List<String> = emptyList()">
        The IDs of the choices in the order they had in releases without an <code>id</code>.
      </Member>
    </Members>
    <p>
      Once a filter has an <code>id</code>, presets saved before it had one find it only through <code>legacyNames</code>, so a source
      that has already shipped filters without metadata lists each filter's old label there, even if the label hasn't changed. A
      <code>Select</code> or <code>Sort</code> also needs <code>legacyOptionIds</code>, or its saved choice is lost:
    </p>
    <Snippet lang="kotlin" code={legacy} />
    <p>
      Katari refuses to search with a filter list whose identities are ambiguous, and shows <strong>Ambiguous filter identity</strong>
      with the filter's name. That happens when:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>An <code>id</code> or alias is blank, or used by two filters.</li>
      <li>
        <code>optionIds</code> doesn't have exactly one non-blank, distinct ID per choice, or the state points past the last choice.
      </li>
      <li><code>neutralOptionId</code>, or a target in <code>optionAliases</code>, isn't one of <code>optionIds</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A text value is saved as it is. If its format changes, as with a <code>Text</code>, <code>Autocomplete</code>,
      <code>EntryDateFilter</code> or <code>PagedGroup</code> state, implement <code>EntryFilterValueMigration</code> on the filter. Values saved
      before it have version 1, so raise <code>filterStateVersion</code> to 2 and above, and convert older values in
      <code>migrateFilterValue(value, previousVersion)</code>, or return null for ones that can't be converted. Katari also passes values
      saved at the current version, so return those unchanged. Without it, only values
      from version 1 restore. A preset whose value can't be restored, or fails <a href="#validation">validation</a> once restored, asks
      the user for repair.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="counting" title="Active filters and clearing">
    <p>The search sheet shows how many filters are active and offers to clear them:</p>
    <table class="terms">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Filter</th> <th>Counts as active</th> <th>Clearing</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>CheckBox</code>, <code>TriState</code></td> <td data-label="Counts as active">When on, or not ignored.</td> <td data-label="Clearing">Turns it off, or back to ignored.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>EntryDateFilter</code></td> <td data-label="Counts as active">When it holds a date.</td> <td data-label="Clearing">Empties it, unless it is required.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>Select</code> with <code>neutralOptionId</code></td>
          <td data-label="Counts as active">When another choice is picked.</td>
          <td data-label="Clearing">Goes back to the neutral choice, such as "Any".</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>Sort</code>, and a <code>Select</code> with <code>role = ORDERING</code></td>
          <td data-label="Counts as active">Never. They are shown with the sort controls.</td>
          <td data-label="Clearing">Left alone.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code>PagedGroup</code></td> <td data-label="Counts as active">Its <code>selectedItemCount</code>.</td> <td data-label="Clearing">Not offered.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Group</code></td> <td data-label="Counts as active">The sum of the filters inside.</td> <td data-label="Clearing">Clears every filter inside, if each of them can be cleared.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>Text</code>, <code>Autocomplete</code>, <code>Select</code> without <code>neutralOptionId</code></td>
          <td data-label="Counts as active">Katari can't tell, and neither can a group holding one.</td>
          <td data-label="Clearing">Not offered.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      For a filter Katari can't count, implement <code>EntryFilterStateSemantics</code>, as in <a href="#rare">Rarely needed</a>.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="dates" title="Dates">
    <p>
      <code>EntryDateFilter</code> opens a date editor instead of a text field. It takes a year, a month or a full date, and needs an
      <code>id</code> in its metadata:
    </p>
    <Snippet lang="kotlin" code={dates} />
    <Members>
      <Member signature="initialValue: EntryPartialDate? = null">The date the filter starts with, or null for an empty one.</Member>
      <Member signature="allowedPrecisions: Set<EntryDatePrecision> = setOf(YEAR, MONTH, DAY)">
        Which of the <code>EntryDatePrecision</code> values the user can pick. It must not be empty.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="minimum: EntryPartialDate? = null">The earliest date allowed, or null for no limit.</Member>
      <Member signature="maximum: EntryPartialDate? = null">The latest date allowed, or null for no limit.</Member>
      <Member signature="required: Boolean = false">When true, an empty date fails validation.</Member>
    </Members>
    <p>
      Read the picked date from <code>dateValue</code>, an <code>EntryPartialDate(year, month, day)</code> whose month and day are null
      when the user didn't pick them, or null when it is empty. Its <code>toString()</code> gives <code>2024</code>,
      <code>2024-05</code> or <code>2024-05-17</code>. Years run from 1 to 9999, and <code>EntryPartialDate.parse</code> returns null for
      text that isn't such a date.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="validation" title="Required and related fields">
    <p>
      An <code>EntryDateFilter</code> already rejects dates outside its limits, and an empty one when <code>required = true</code>. For a
      rule across several filters, such as a range whose end must not come before its start, implement
      <code>EntryFilterValidator</code> on the group that holds them. Katari passes copies of the group's filters to
      <code>validateFilter</code>. They carry the values but aren't your own classes, so read them as <code>EntryDateFilter</code>,
      <code>EntryFilter.Text</code> and so on, and don't change them:
    </p>
    <Snippet lang="kotlin" code={range} />
    <p>
      Each <code>EntryFilterValidationIssue(filterIds, code, message)</code> names the IDs of the filters it is about. Katari marks those
      filters, lists the problems at the top of the sheet, and won't apply the filters or save a preset until they are fixed, so
      <code>getSearchContent</code> only receives valid values. Katari words the problem from its <code>code</code>:
      <code>REQUIRED</code>, <code>INVALID_DATE</code>, <code>DATE_PRECISION</code>, <code>DATE_BOUNDS</code> or
      <code>DATE_RANGE</code>. For a rule of your own, use <code>SOURCE</code> and give the text as <code>message</code>; a <code>message</code> given with any
      other code replaces Katari's wording too.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="site-options" title="Options the site provides">
    <p>
      A <code>Group</code> lists its choices in your code. When the site has too many to list, or they change too often, let Katari ask
      the site instead:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong><code>Autocomplete</code></strong> is a text field that suggests values from the site as the user types, such as author
        names. Its state is still the plain text, so a preset never depends on a suggestion being found again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong><code>PagedGroup</code></strong> is a list of choices that Katari loads page by page, with a search field, such as a
        site's thousands of tags. It opens on a screen of its own and can show what is selected separately.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      An <code>Autocomplete</code> decides what part of the text to look up, fetches suggestions, and returns the text after the user
      picks one. Katari schedules the lookups and cancels those that are no longer needed:
    </p>
    <Snippet lang="kotlin" code={autocomplete} />
    <Members>
      <Member signature="fun getSuggestionQuery(input: EntryFilterTextInput): String?">
        Returns the text to look up from <code>input</code>'s <code>text</code>, <code>selectionStart</code> and <code>selectionEnd</code>,
        or null when suggestions don't apply where the cursor is. The selection is in UTF-16 indices and its ends may be reversed.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="suspend fun getSuggestions(input: EntryFilterTextInput, query: String): List<EntryFilterSuggestion>">
        Returns <code>EntryFilterSuggestion(id, label, value = id)</code>s. <code>id</code> and <code>label</code> must not be empty.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="fun applySuggestion(input: EntryFilterTextInput, suggestion: EntryFilterSuggestion): EntryFilterTextEdit">
        Returns the whole new text and cursor as <code>EntryFilterTextEdit(text, selectionStart, selectionEnd = selectionStart)</code>,
        so the source keeps its own separators, quotes and other syntax.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="val options: EntryFilterAutocompleteOptions">
        Passed to the constructor.
        <Members label="Fields">
          <Member signature="debounceMillis: Long = 300">How long after the last change Katari waits before asking.</Member>
          <Member signature="minimumQueryLength: Int = 1">Shorter text isn't looked up.</Member>
          <Member signature="requestOnFocus: Boolean = false">Ask as soon as the field is focused, before the user types.</Member>
          <Member signature="maximumResults: Int = 20">How many suggestions are shown.</Member>
        </Members>
      </Member>
    </Members>
  </Task>

  <Task id="paged-group" title="Lists loaded page by page">
    <p>
      A <code>PagedGroup&lt;S&gt;(name, initialState, options)</code> keeps the user's choices in one immutable state of your own type
      <code>S</code>. The items Katari loads, and the controls made from them, are only views of that state, and Katari throws them away
      as the user scrolls:
    </p>
    <Snippet lang="kotlin" code={paged} />
    <Members>
      <Member signature="suspend fun getPage(request: EntryFilterPageRequest): EntryFilterPage">
        Returns one page as <code>EntryFilterPage(items, nextContinuationToken, previousContinuationToken)</code>. The request's
        <code>scope</code> is <code>AVAILABLE</code> for everything, or <code>SELECTED</code> for what the state holds; <code>query</code>
        is the user's search or null; <code>continuationToken</code> is the token you returned for the next or previous page, or null for
        the first; <code>requestedSize</code> is the page size. A null next token ends the list. Each item is an
        <code>EntryFilterPageItem(id, label, value = id)</code> whose <code>id</code> stays the same for the same item on every page.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="fun projectItem(item: EntryFilterPageItem, previous: EntryFilter<*>?): EntryFilter<*>">
        Returns the control shown for an item, such as a <code>TriState</code>, set from the state. <code>previous</code> is the control
        last made for the same item while it stays visible, which can be updated and returned instead.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="fun reduceItemUpdate(item: EntryFilterPageItem, updatedFilter: EntryFilter<*>): S">
        Returns a new state with the user's change to that control.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="fun selectedItemCount(state: S): Int">How many choices the state holds, for the active count.</Member>
      <Member signature="fun encodeState(state: S): String">
        Turns the state into the text a preset saves. It must give the same text for the same state.
      </Member>
      <Member signature="fun decodeState(value: String): S?">Reads saved text back into a state, or returns null for text it can't read.</Member>
      <Member signature="val options: EntryFilterPagingOptions">
        Passed to the constructor.
        <Members label="Fields">
          <Member signature="pageSize: Int = 50">At most 200.</Member>
          <Member signature="search: EntryFilterPagingSearchOptions?">
            <code>EntryFilterPagingSearchOptions(debounceMillis = 300, minimumQueryLength = 1)</code> by default, or null for no search
            field.
          </Member>
        </Members>
      </Member>
      <Member signature="open suspend fun getNavigation(request: EntryFilterNavigationRequest): EntryFilterNavigation">
        Optional. Returns <code>EntryFilterNavigationTarget(id, label, anchor)</code>s, such as letters of the alphabet, for jumping
        through a long list. Katari passes a picked target's <code>anchor</code> as the next request's <code>initialAnchor</code>, and an
        item's <code>navigationTargetId</code> tells Katari where the list has scrolled to.
      </Member>
    </Members>
    <Callout label="Keep requests cheap">
      <p>
        Suggestions and pages are fetched while the user types and scrolls. Use the site's own lookup endpoint where it has one, and the
        same rate limit as the rest of the source. See <a href={sitePath("docs/extensions/source/#network")}>Requests</a>.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Task>

  <Task id="rare" title="Rarely needed">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>EntryFilterGroupSummary</code> on a <code>Group</code> replaces the text under it while it is collapsed:
        <code>selectionSummary(values)</code> returns the text for the filters inside, or null to keep Katari's.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>EntryFilterStateSemantics</code> tells Katari how to count and clear a filter whose state it can't read, such as a
        <code>Text</code> holding a whole query. <code>activeSelectionCount(value)</code> returns the count, or null when it can't tell;
        <code>canClearSelection</code> says whether clearing is offered; <code>clearSelection(value)</code> clears it. Read and change
        only the <code>value</code> Katari passes, never the filter's own state.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Task>
</GuidePage>
